[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO] {
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]   "type": "assistant",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]   "message": {
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]     "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]     "id": "msg_011CfiA8Q94k8LkZBdZqi8uj",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]     "type": "message",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]     "role": "assistant",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]     "content": [
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]       {
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]         "type": "text",
[2026-10-05T00:39:56.869Z] [INFO]         "text": "I found three asymmetries that affect results: C# timings are measured before the JIT has finished optimising, and both Doublets stores use different index structures in C# than in Rust. Several smaller differences follow. The dataset, SQLite durability settings, repetition logic and JSON schema all match. Another process edited files while I was reviewing (harness.rs, Harness.cs, Objects.cs, Links.cs and others). Line numbers are from the current tree, and none of those edits change the findings.\n\n**Real issues (verified)**\n\n1. **C# results are measured before the JIT has optimised the code, so they depend on variant order.** `csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Harness.cs:261` warms up on only `min(n, 10000)` records. The .NET runtime first runs code unoptimised and only swaps in optimised code after about 100 ms without new compilation. On `objects 32 300`, United_Volatile_Cached create took 424/340/332 µs with defaults, but 74/72/71 µs with `DOTNET_TieredCompilation=0`. SQLite_Memory read_all dropped from about 13 µs to about 3.5 µs. Whichever variant runs first absorbs the compilation cost, which made C# Volatile look about 7× slower than NonVolatile. Rust is compiled ahead of time, so the languages aren't measured the same way. This affects PR runs (size 1000) most. It also affects 10M runs, which have only one sample. Fix: disable tiered compilation in the csproj, or warm up for a fixed time instead of a record count.\n\n2. **The United store uses a different index tree in each language.** `Harness.cs:310` forces C# United to `SizedAndThreadedAVLBalancedTree` for links too, not just objects (the code comment only justifies it for objects). Rust `unit::Store` uses `LinksSources/TargetsRecursionlessSizeBalancedTree` (doublets-0.5.0 `src/mem/unit/store.rs:28-29`).\n\n3. **The Split store also differs.** C# `SplitMemoryLinks` (`Harness.cs:313`) is built with `useLinkedList=true`, so internal sources sit in a linked list (I checked this by reflection). Rust's split store has `USE_LIST = false` and uses `InternalSourcesRecursionlessTree` (`split/store.rs:61`). Rows labelled \"Doublets_Split\" therefore measure different data structures.\n\n4. **C# SQLite starts transactions with `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`, Rust with deferred `BEGIN`.** C# calls `BeginTransaction()` at `SQLiteStorage.cs:43`; Rust uses `\"BEGIN\"` at `links.rs:170` and `objects.rs:114`. I confirmed C# takes the write lock immediately with a second connection. Read-only batches pay for a lock they don't need. The effect is small.\n\n5. **SQLite create uses different SQL.** C# runs `INSERT … RETURNING id` (`Links.cs:50`, `Objects.cs:42`). Rust runs a plain INSERT and then reads `last_insert_rowid()` (`links.rs:91-94`, `objects.rs:65-71`). Each language's SQLite does slightly different work.\n\n6. **Timed regions include different language-side overhead:**\n   - C# allocates a UTF-8 copy for every checksum (`Dataset.cs:113`, `Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes`); Rust doesn't.\n   - Rust copies about 800 bytes of content per generated post (`dataset.rs:85`, `to_owned()`); C# reuses the same string.\n   - Rust's cached `string()` clones on every hit (`objects.rs:213`); C# returns the same reference.\n\n   These add a constant to every Doublets and SQLite read/create and skew comparisons between the languages.\n\n7. **Rust does extra Doublets updates that C# doesn't.** `links.rs:207-211` adds a reset-to-(0,0) update when a link would reference itself. That happens for about 2·ln(n) links, so the time impact is negligible, but the code paths differ.\n\n**Fairness notes (same in both languages)**\n\n- Doublets \"transactions\" do nothing, and the memory-mapped stores never flush to disk. SQLite_File runs with `synchronous=FULL` and `journal_mode=DELETE`, so it pays for durable commits on every write batch. `benchmark_report.py:131-135` still sets Doublets_NonVolatile against SQLite_File as if they had the same durability.\n- The \"File size\" column shows Doublets' reserved memory map (32–84 MiB even for 2,000 links) next to SQLite's actual file size. That's misleading.\n\n**Uncertain**\n\n- Microsoft.Data.Sqlite probably opens connections in serialized mode, while rusqlite uses `SQLITE_OPEN_NO_MUTEX` (`rusqlite lib.rs:1261-1264`). That would add a small per-call locking cost in C#. I didn't confirm the C# flags.\n- An empty string is handled differently: Rust returns the sequence marker (`objects.rs:168`), while C#'s library converter behaviour is unknown. It doesn't affect the benchmark data, only the round-trip test.\n\n**Verified as matching**\n\n- **Dataset:** splitmix64, `link`, `mix`, `scattered`, blog posts and checksums use the same algorithms. The five paragraphs are byte-identical (checked with diff). C# arithmetic is unchecked, so it wraps like Rust's.\n- **SQLite settings:** neither language sets pragmas. Both libraries default to `journal_mode=delete`, `synchronous=2`, 4 KiB pages and `cache_size=-2000`; I confirmed this at runtime for C# and from the build flags for Rust. The versions differ slightly: 3.53.2 (Rust, bundled) vs 3.53.3 (e_sqlite3). The schema and indices `(from,to)`/`(to,from)` are identical.\n- **Repetitions and warm-up:** the same `clamp(work/size, 1, 10)` formula, one discarded warm-up of `min(n, 10000)`, and ns-per-op computed by dividing by n.\n- **Lifecycle:** the same operations, `scattered` order, Tally checks and post-update/post-delete checks. Store setup and file-size measurement are outside the timed regions.\n- **JSON:** both write the same keys, and file sizes match (102400 for SQLite, 33554432/41943040 for 32-bit Doublets). Rust sorts operation keys alphabetically, which the script ignores because it reads them through `OPERATIONS`.\n\nThe tiny runs (links 2000, objects 300, both bit widths, both languages) passed every checksum. Their outputs are in `/tmp/rv/*.json`, and the throwaway reflection program I used for the C# checks is in `/tmp/rv/refl`."
