[2026-10-05T00:43:03.760Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n     - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791159559061.\n     - Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b. Push only to this branch.\n     - PR #108 already exists: update it with `gh pr edit`, never create a new one, and finish with `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Latest user feedback, PR comment by konard (verbatim): \"We need to recover from https://gist.githubusercontent.com/konard/b7b614bf7feef932de7864cff3a5d710/raw/7324688fe747447f55d7854b33961d59d0ffc64a/tmp-hive-mind-log-upload-OLcnzK-sanitized.log.txt, double check that all CI/CD checks will pass like CodeFactor and so on, and that https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 is fully addressed.\"\n   - Issue #107 requirements:\n     - README hierarchy: category → language (Rust/C# doublets vs SQLite) → \"32/64 bit address/id space benchmarks\" → size.\n     - SQLite `links(id, from, to)` with indices and the same id type in both languages.\n     - Fully symmetrical coverage.\n     - Objects benchmarks with and without a sequences cache.\n     - All dependencies at their latest versions.\n     - Short, self-explanatory code with few comments.\n     - No false positives or negatives.\n     - The largest sizes that fit in 6-hour CI jobs, splitting jobs if needed.\n     - Best practices. \"Make it perfect\".\n   - Security constraint: the user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) may be used only to identify the user (authorship/attribution). It must never be sent to an unrelated service in a request header, URL or payload unless the user explicitly asks.\n   - Guidelines:\n     - Put experiments in ./experiments and save CI logs in ci-logs/.\n     - Make atomic commits and run local checks before pushing.\n     - Write the PR description in PR #106 style: \"Fixes #107\" first; sections \"What changed\", \"Reproduction and regression coverage\", \"Verification\"; a no-UI closing sentence.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Rust: doublets 0.5.0, platform-mem 0.3.0, rusqlite 0.40.2 (bundled SQLite 3.53.2), edition 2024. The `memory::Whole` wrapper works around the grow bug.\n   - C#: net10.0, Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.12, Platform.Data.Doublets 0.18.1, Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.6.5, xunit.v3.\n   - GitHub Actions (all latest): checkout@v7, setup-python@v7, setup-dotnet@v6, upload-artifact@v7, download-artifact@v8.\n   - Benchmarks workflow:\n     - PR runs use size [1000]; push to main uses [100000, 1000000, 10000000].\n     - Objects at 10M and 100M are excluded.\n     - timeout-minutes is 355, and the report job publishes the README as LinksPlatformBencher.\n   - Codacy enforces markdownlint MD013/MD022/MD024, a subset of SonarC# and Prospector pydocstyle D213 on new issues.\n     - Its PR issues API is public: https://app.codacy.com/api/v3/analysis/organizations/gh/linksplatform/repositories/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/pull-requests/108/issues?status=new&limit=100. The last fetch showed 65 issues, all now addressed locally.\n   - CodeFactor uses StyleCop SA1407 and complexity checks, and was green.\n   - Local tools:\n     - markdownlint-cli 0.49.1 at /tmp/mdl/node_modules/.bin/markdownlint;\n     - a Python venv at /tmp/venv (matplotlib 3.11.2, pydocstyle);\n     - a scratch C# copy at /tmp/sc whose Directory.Build.props now references SonarAnalyzer.CSharp.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **README.md / README.ru.md** (committed in 4e9cec4):\n     - The H1 comes first, then each badge on its own line.\n     - Prose is wrapped to 80 columns, and bare URLs use `<...>`.\n     - The run-locally block now reads:\n       ```\n       cargo run --release --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml -- \\\n         links 64 100000 --output results/links-rust-64-100000.json\n       dotnet run -c Release --project csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets -- \\\n         objects 32 1000 --variants SQLite_File,Doublets_Split_NonVolatile_Cached\n       # print the tables, add --readme README.md --charts docs/benchmarks to update\n       python3 scripts/benchmark_report.py results\n       ```\n     - Original comparison: blank lines after the headings, fences and table. `<!-- markdownlint-disable MD013 MD060 -->` sits before `### SQLite`, and `<!-- markdownlint-restore -->` before `### Conclusion`. The Conclusion is wrapped.\n     - The generated section is wrapped in LINT_OFF/LINT_ON, and its table separators read `| --- | ---: |`.\n     - Both files pass markdownlint with all default rules.\n     - The CI-sizes sentence still says: links 100k/1M/10M, objects 100k/1M, larger sizes don't fit. It may need an update after the 100M probe.\n   - **scripts/benchmark_report.py** (committed):\n     ```python\n     END_MARKER = \"<!--BENCHMARK_RESULTS_END-->\"\n     # The wide tables cannot be wrapped, and the hierarchy repeats headings under different parents.\n     LINT_OFF = \"<!-- markdownlint-disable MD013 MD024 -->\"\n     LINT_ON = \"<!-- markdownlint-restore -->\"\n     ...\n     lines = [\"| \" + \" | \".join(header) + \" |\", \"| --- |\" + \" ---: |\" * (len(header) - 1)]\n     ...\n     return pattern.sub(lambda _: f\"{START_MARKER}\\n{LINT_OFF}\\n{generated}{LINT_ON}\\n{END_MARKER}\", document)\n     ...\n     parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__.strip().splitlines()[0])\n     ```\n     The module docstring and the `comparison` docstring now start with `\"\"\"\\n` (D213).\n   - **scripts/test_benchmark_report.py** (committed), 17 tests:\n     - added the separator assertion;\n     - the replace_section expectation includes `{report.LINT_OFF}`/`{report.LINT_ON}`;\n     - added `test_usage_starts_with_the_summary`, which calls `report.main([\"--help\"])` with redirect_stdout and asserts the summary text appears.\n   - **.github/workflows/benchmarks.yml** (committed):\n     - report-tests job: added `- run: npx --yes markdownlint-cli@0.49.1 README.md README.ru.md`.\n     - PR \"Check the report\" step:\n       ```\n       run: |\n         python scripts/benchmark_report.py \"$RUNNER_TEMP/results\" >> \"$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY\"\n         python scripts/benchmark_report.py \"$RUNNER_TEMP/results\" --readme README.md --readme README.ru.md\n         npx --yes markdownlint-cli@0.49.1 README.md README.ru.md\n       ```\n   - **UNCOMMITTED C#/Rust Sonar fixes** (all local checks pass):\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Objects.cs: removed the `_unicodeSequence` field; it is now `var unicodeSequence = Marker();`, used in both converters.\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Links.cs: `private static Link<T> Link(IList<T> values) => new(values[0], values[1], values[2]);`\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Harness.cs:\n       - `public static ulong WarmUpSize => 10_000;`\n       - `United<T>(IResizableDirectMemory memory, bool external)` and `Split<T>(data, index, bool external)` take a required parameter. The links call sites pass `external: false`, and the objects call sites keep `external: true`.\n       - Update loop: `var (newTo, newFrom) = Link(id); storage.Update(Int(id), Int(newFrom), Int(newTo)); tally.Add(LinkChecksum(id, newFrom, newTo));`\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Dataset.cs (LinksChecksum): `var (from, to) = Link(i); checksum += LinkChecksum(i, swapped ? to : from, swapped ? from : to);`\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Program.cs:\n       ```\n       var defaultWork = category == \"links\" ? LinksWork : ObjectsWork;\n       var work = options.TryGetValue(\"work\", out var workText) ? Number(workText) : defaultWork;\n       ```\n     - csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets.Tests/StoragesTests.cs:53 passes `external: false`.\n     - rust/src/harness.rs update loop: `let (new_to, new_from) = link(id); links.update(int(id), int(new_from), int(new_to)); tally.add(dataset::link_checksum(id, new_from, new_to));`\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - Codacy reported 65 issues. Fixed as described above and verified locally with markdownlint, pydocstyle and SonarAnalyzer.\n   - The D213 docstring change broke the argparse description, because `__doc__.splitlines()[0]` became empty. Fixed with `.strip()`, and a regression test was added and shown to fail without the fix.\n   - A stash-based check proved nothing, because stashing also removed the new test. Replaced it with a targeted sed revert check.\n   - The SonarAnalyzer build reports warnings as errors because of TreatWarningsAsErrors. I parsed the error lines instead.\n   - StyleCop showed one new SA1513 from an `if` block. Replaced the block with ternary arguments; CodeFactor doesn't enforce SA1513 anyway.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Remaining Sonar findings that Codacy doesn't enforce, deliberately not fixed:\n     - S1215 `GC.Collect` (intentional between benchmark repetitions);\n     - S6966 sync I/O in the CLI;\n     - S2077 table name from an internal field;\n     - S3881 dispose pattern (boilerplate).\n   - The 100M links probe run 37228604862 (workflow_dispatch, 4 jobs started 20:00Z) was still in progress at 00:32Z. It will finish or time out around 01:55Z.\n   - The background review agent completed. Findings to evaluate and fix where real:\n     1. C# tiered JIT: the warm-up on min(n, 10k) is too short, so results depend on variant order (create 424→74 µs with `DOTNET_TieredCompilation=0`). Suggested fix: `<TieredCompilation>false</TieredCompilation>` in the csproj, or a time-based warm-up.\n     2. The C# United store uses `IndexTreeType.SizedAndThreadedAVLBalancedTree` for links too, while Rust uses recursionless size-balanced trees.\n     3. C# `SplitMemoryLinks` uses `useLinkedList=true`, while Rust split has `USE_LIST=false` and an internal sources tree.\n     4. C# `BeginTransaction()` takes the write lock immediately (`BEGIN IMMEDIATE`), while Rust uses deferred `\"BEGIN\"` (links.rs:170, objects.rs:114).\n     5. C# uses `INSERT … RETURNING id` (Links.cs:50, Objects.cs:42), while Rust uses `last_insert_rowid()` (links.rs:91-94, objects.rs:65-71).\n     6. Overhead inside the timed regions: the C# checksum allocates a UTF-8 copy (Dataset.cs:113), Rust copies the content with `to_owned()` (dataset.rs:85), and Rust's cached string is cloned on every hit (objects.rs:213).\n     7. Rust's extra reset update for self-references; negligible.\n     - Fairness notes:\n       - Doublets NonVolatile never flushes, while SQLite_File runs with synchronous=FULL.\n       - The file size column shows the Doublets preallocation.\n     - Verified as matching: dataset, SQLite pragmas, repetitions, lifecycle and JSON.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The initial prompt gave the issue, branch, working directory and PR 108. It said: \"New comments on the pull request: 1\", \"Pull request description was edited after last commit\", \"Merge status is UNSTABLE\", \"Failed pull request checks: 1\", \"Continue.\"\n   - konard's PR comment, quoted verbatim in section 1.\n   - System requests for brief status updates; I answered with short progress notes.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Commit the uncommitted Sonar fixes, e.g. \"Fix Codacy SonarC# issues …\", and push.\n   - Evaluate the review agent's findings and fix the real asymmetries, documenting any I decide against in the PR:\n     - TieredCompilation off, or a better warm-up;\n     - aligning the index tree types and the split linked list where the libraries allow;\n     - matching the SQLite transaction mode and insert SQL across languages;\n     - minimising non-storage overhead in the timed regions.\n   - Then re-run the local checks:\n     - Rust: `cargo fmt --check`, clippy with `-D warnings`, `cargo test --release`;\n     - C#: build with `-warnaserror` and `dotnet test`;\n     - Python: unittest via /tmp/venv;\n     - markdownlint.\n   - Check the 100M probe run 37228604862. Download the logs of any failed jobs to ci-logs/. Decide on 100M coverage and update the workflow default sizes and README text (en and ru). Pull any results commit the bot pushes; `git pull --rebase` before pushing.\n   - Verify all PR checks are green: Rust, csharp, Benchmarks, CodeFactor, Codacy. Re-query the Codacy API after the push.\n   - Check for new issue and PR comments.\n   - Update the PR #108 title and description in PR #106 style, covering:\n     - the regressions and their coverage: the split self-reference bug, C# `Delete(id, handler: null)`, AVL degeneration, `memory::Whole`, the report IQR false negative, the 1e+03 formatting bug;\n     - the Codacy and CodeFactor fixes;\n     - the run URLs 37226110636 and 37228604862;\n     - the test counts (C# 28, Rust 9, Python 17);\n     - the no-UI sentence.\n   - Final checks: `git status` clean, main merged, review `gh pr diff`, then `gh pr ready 108`.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished the C#/Rust Sonar fixes and run the full local checks, which all pass:\n   - C#: 0 warnings, 0 errors, 28/28 tests;\n   - Rust: fmt OK, clippy clean, 1+8 tests;\n   - Python: OK.\n   These changes are not committed yet. Commit 4e9cec4 (the README/markdownlint/D213 work) is committed but not pushed. Then the review agent's report arrived, listing asymmetries between Rust and C#.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Commit the pending Sonar fixes and push. This continues the user's request to \"double check that all CI/CD checks will pass like CodeFactor and so on\":\n   ```\n   git add -A csharp rust\n   git commit -m \"Fix Codacy SonarC# issues …\"\n   git push origin issue-107-bce58c21006b\n   ```\n   Then address the review agent's verified asymmetries (JIT warm-up, index tree types, transaction mode, insert SQL). This serves the requirement that the issue be \"fully addressed\" with fully symmetrical, false-positive-free benchmarks. After that, check run 37228604862.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code