[2026-10-05T01:15:07.028Z] [INFO]         "text": "This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Solve GitHub issue https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107.\n     - Working directory: /tmp/gh-issue-solver-1791159559061.\n     - Branch: issue-107-bce58c21006b. Push only to this branch.\n     - PR #108 already exists: update it with `gh pr edit`, never create a new one, and finish with `gh pr ready 108`.\n   - Latest user feedback, PR comment by konard (verbatim): \"We need to recover from https://gist.githubusercontent.com/konard/b7b614bf7feef932de7864cff3a5d710/raw/7324688fe747447f55d7854b33961d59d0ffc64a/tmp-hive-mind-log-upload-OLcnzK-sanitized.log.txt, double check that all CI/CD checks will pass like CodeFactor and so on, and that https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 is fully addressed.\"\n   - Issue #107 requirements:\n     - README hierarchy: category → language (Rust/C# doublets vs SQLite) → \"32/64 bit address/id space benchmarks\" → size.\n     - SQLite `links(id, from, to)` with indices and the same id type in both languages.\n     - Fully symmetrical coverage.\n     - Objects benchmarks with and without a sequences cache.\n     - All dependencies at their latest versions.\n     - Short, self-explanatory code with few comments.\n     - No false positives or negatives.\n     - The largest sizes that fit in 6-hour CI jobs, splitting jobs if needed.\n     - Best practices. \"Make it perfect\".\n   - Security constraint: the user's email (link.assistant.team@proton.me) may be used only to identify the user (authorship/attribution). It must never be sent to an unrelated service in a request header, URL or payload unless the user explicitly asks.\n   - Guidelines:\n     - Put experiments in ./experiments and save CI logs in ci-logs/.\n     - Make atomic commits and run local checks before pushing.\n     - Write the PR description in PR #106 style: \"Fixes #107\" first; sections \"What changed\", \"Reproduction and regression coverage\", \"Verification\"; a no-UI closing sentence.\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Rust: doublets 0.5.0 (the split store has `USE_LIST=false`), platform-mem 0.3.0, rusqlite 0.40.2 (bundled SQLite 3.53.2; `FromSql`/`ToSql` support `Arc<str>`), edition 2024.\n   - C#: net10.0, Microsoft.Data.Sqlite 10.0.12.\n     - `BeginTransaction()` sends `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`.\n     - `SqliteConnection.Handle` plus `SQLitePCL.raw.sqlite3_last_insert_rowid`.\n   - C# libraries: Platform.Data.Doublets 0.18.1 (the `SplitMemoryLinks` default is `useLinkedList=true`; the united store uses AVL trees because the default SBT degenerates), Platform.Data.Doublets.Sequences 0.6.5, xunit.v3.\n   - SQLite `INSERT … RETURNING id` is about 3× slower than execute + `last_insert_rowid` (≈6.0 vs 2.0 µs per insert).\n   - .NET tiered JIT and dynamic PGO:\n     - With TC=0, results are consistent but steady state is about 2× slower.\n     - The fix is a time-based warm-up of at least 1 second.\n   - Codacy enforces markdownlint, part of SonarC# and pydocstyle D213. CodeFactor enforces StyleCop and complexity checks.\n     - The public Codacy API endpoint for new PR issues: https://app.codacy.com/api/v3/analysis/organizations/gh/linksplatform/repositories/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/pull-requests/108/issues?status=new&limit=100\n   - Local tools:\n     - markdownlint at /tmp/mdl/node_modules/.bin/markdownlint;\n     - a Python venv at /tmp/venv;\n     - a Sonar scratch copy at /tmp/sc/csharp. Copy files in with `for f in $(git ls-files csharp | grep \"\\.cs$\"); do cp $f /tmp/sc/$f; done`; there is no rsync.\n   - C# tests are run as in CI: `cd csharp && dotnet build -c Release -warnaserror && dotnet test --project SQLiteVSDoublets.Tests -c Release --no-build`.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **Commit 1346e64 (pushed):** the Sonar fixes (required `external` flag, `WarmUpSize` property, local unicodeSequence, named swapped link ends, a separate default-work statement).\n   - **csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/SQLiteStorage.cs (staged):** adds `using SQLitePCL;` and:\n     ```csharp\n     /// <summary>Like <c>RETURNING id</c>, but without its cost (3× slower inserts, see experiments/sqlite_returning).\n     protected long LastInsertRowId => raw.sqlite3_last_insert_rowid(_connection.Handle);\n     ```\n   - **csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Links.cs (staged):** the insert SQL no longer has `RETURNING id`.\n     ```csharp\n     public T Create(T from, T to)\n     {\n         With(_create, from, to).ExecuteNonQuery();\n         return Id(LastInsertRowId);\n     }\n     ```\n   - **csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Objects.cs (staged):** the insert SQL no longer has `RETURNING id`, and Create ends with `_create.ExecuteNonQuery(); return T.CreateChecked(LastInsertRowId);`.\n   - **rust/src/links.rs (staged):** `execute_batch(\"BEGIN IMMEDIATE\")`. Create is back to `statement.execute(params![sql(from), sql(to)]).unwrap(); id(self.connection.last_insert_rowid())`.\n   - **rust/src/objects.rs:**\n     - Staged: only the BEGIN IMMEDIATE hunk.\n     - Unstaged: the `Arc<str>` changes.\n       - `use std::{..., sync::Arc}`.\n       - SequencesCache uses `HashMap<Arc<str>, T>` and `HashMap<T, Arc<str>>`.\n       - `fn sequence(&mut self, string: &Arc<str>)` looks up with `.get(&**string)` and inserts `Arc::clone(string)`.\n       - `fn string(...) -> Arc<str>` returns `Arc::clone(string)` on a cache hit and `Arc::default()` for the empty sequence; otherwise it builds `String::from_utf16(&utf16).unwrap().into()`.\n       - Create is unchanged and uses last_insert_rowid.\n   - **experiments/sqlite_returning/{Cargo.toml, src/main.rs} (staged):** a standalone rusqlite probe timing 100,000 inserts per method (execute + last_insert_rowid, query_row RETURNING, query + drain). It has its own `[workspace]`; target/ is gitignored.\n   - **rust/src/dataset.rs (unstaged):**\n     - `use std::sync::{Arc, LazyLock};`\n     - BlogPost doc: \"Strings are shared like in C#, so neither the dataset nor a sequences cache copies them.\" Fields `title`/`content` are `Arc<str>`.\n     - `static CONTENTS: LazyLock<[Arc<str>; 5]> = LazyLock::new(|| PARAGRAPHS.map(Arc::from));`\n     - `blog_post` uses `format!(\"Blog post {i}\").into()` and `Arc::clone(&CONTENTS[(i % 5) as usize])`.\n   - **rust/tests/storages.rs (unstaged):** `title: \"\".into()` instead of `String::new()`.\n   - **csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Dataset.cs (unstaged):** allocation-free BlogPost.Bytes.\n     ```csharp\n     ulong sum = 0;\n     Span<byte> utf8 = stackalloc byte[4];\n     foreach (var rune in text.EnumerateRunes())\n     {\n         foreach (var b in utf8[..rune.EncodeToUtf8(utf8)])\n         {\n             sum += b;\n         }\n     }\n     return sum;\n     ```\n   - **csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Harness.cs (unstaged):**\n     - Added `public static TimeSpan WarmUpTime => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1);`.\n     - Measure now has a local `(List<(string, TimeSpan)>, ulong) Run(ulong size)` using workspace `Path.Combine(directory, variant)`.\n     - Warm-up: `var warmUp = Stopwatch.StartNew(); do { Run(Math.Min(n, WarmUpSize)); } while (warmUp.Elapsed < WarmUpTime);`\n     - Then repetitions 1..repetitions call `Run(n)`.\n     - The doc says \"after discarded warm-ups that run for at least <see cref=\"WarmUpTime\"/>, so the JIT has optimized the code.\"\n   - **rust/src/harness.rs (unstaged):**\n     - Added `pub const WARM_UP_TIME: Duration = Duration::from_secs(1);`.\n     - measure has a `run` closure returning `(timings, file_bytes)`.\n     - Warm-up: `let warm_up = Instant::now(); run(n.min(WARM_UP_SIZE)); while warm_up.elapsed() < WARM_UP_TIME { run(n.min(WARM_UP_SIZE)); }`, then `for repetition in 1..=repetitions`.\n   - **rust/src/main.rs and csharp/SQLiteVSDoublets/Program.cs (unstaged):** both emit `\"warm_up_seconds\"`, from `harness::WARM_UP_TIME.as_secs_f64()` and `Harness.WarmUpTime.TotalSeconds`.\n   - **README.md (unstaged):**\n     - Warm-up text: \"Every repetition runs on a fresh store in an empty directory, after discarded warm-up repetitions on up to 10,000 records that run for at least a second, so the .NET JIT has already optimized the code.\"\n     - New note before the \"Objects stores use external references…\" bullet: \"The C# split stores keep the default linked list of the internal sources, which doublets 0.5.0 (Rust) does not have; it makes the C# updates faster and the other operations take about the same time ([experiments/csharp_split_linked_list](experiments/csharp_split_linked_list)).\"\n   - **README.ru.md (unstaged):** the equivalent Russian warm-up text (\"…после отбрасываемых прогревочных повторов на не более чем 10 000 записей, которые длятся не меньше секунды, чтобы JIT .NET уже оптимизировал код.\") and the Russian split linked-list note.\n   - **experiments/csharp_warm_up_order.sh (untracked):** runs C# twice and prints the medians, so order effects are visible.\n   - **experiments/rust_objects_ab.sh (untracked):** A/B of the working tree vs HEAD using git stash. The variants are overridable with the `VARIANTS` env var.\n   - **experiments/csharp_split_linked_list/{Program.cs, csharp_split_linked_list.csproj} (untracked):**\n     - It reflects `_useLinkedList` (default True) and compares true vs false.\n     - The header records the results: for links at 100k, update is 1093–1120 ns with the list vs 1704–1863 ns without.\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - **`RETURNING id` slowed Rust inserts** (2.6 → 6.6 µs). Verified with experiments/sqlite_returning, then reverted to last_insert_rowid and applied the same approach to C#.\n   - **`/usr/bin/time` is missing.** Used bash `$SECONDS` in the script instead.\n   - **`dotnet test -c Release csharp` fails on .NET 10 with the VSTest error.** Use `dotnet test --project SQLiteVSDoublets.Tests` from csharp/, as CI does.\n   - **There is no rsync for /tmp/sc.** Used a cp loop over `git ls-files`.\n   - **`git apply --cached` reported \"corrupt patch at line 13\".** Splitting the hunks with `re.split(r'(?m)^(?=@@)', s)` fixed it.\n   - **Foreground `sleep` is blocked.** Used until-loops with run_in_background false and a long timeout.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Verified C# order-dependent JIT results and fixed them with a time-based warm-up in both languages.\n   - Found and fixed a C# SQLite false negative caused by `RETURNING`.\n   - Removed non-storage allocations from the timed regions.\n   - Kept the deliberate library choices (C# AVL trees, C# split linked list) and documented them.\n   - Decided not to fix S1215/S2077/S3881/S6966: Codacy doesn't enforce them, and they are intentional.\n   - Probe run 37228604862 (100M links) was still running.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - The initial prompt gave the issue, branch, working directory and PR 108. It said: \"New comments on the pull request: 1\", \"Pull request description was edited after last commit\", \"Merge status is UNSTABLE\", \"Failed pull request checks: 1\", \"Continue.\"\n   - konard's PR comment, quoted verbatim in section 1.\n   - System requests for brief status updates; I answered with short progress notes.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Commit A (staged) and the remaining commits:\n     - B: time-based warm-up (Harness.cs, harness.rs, main.rs, Program.cs, README warm-up text, experiments/csharp_warm_up_order.sh);\n     - C: shared strings / allocation-free checksums (dataset.rs, the rest of objects.rs, storages.rs, Dataset.cs, experiments/rust_objects_ab.sh);\n     - D: the split linked-list experiment and README notes.\n   - Push afterwards, running `git pull --rebase` first.\n   - Check the CI runs on the new commits (Rust, csharp, Benchmarks, CodeFactor, Codacy). Re-query the Codacy API.\n   - Check probe run 37228604862, which should finish or time out around 01:55Z.\n     - Download the logs of any failed jobs to ci-logs/.\n     - Decide on 100M coverage and update the workflow default sizes and the README sizes text (en and ru) if needed.\n   - The published README results came from before these fixes. The next push to main regenerates them; the PR description should mention this.\n   - Check for new issue and PR comments.\n   - Update the PR #108 title and description in PR #106 style, covering:\n     - the regressions and their coverage (split self-reference, C# `Delete(id, handler: null)`, AVL degeneration, `memory::Whole`, the report IQR false negative, the 1e+03 formatting bug);\n     - the new fixes (RETURNING → last_insert_rowid, BEGIN IMMEDIATE, the JIT warm-up, shared strings, the split linked-list note);\n     - the Codacy and CodeFactor fixes;\n     - the run URLs 37226110636 and 37228604862;\n     - the test counts (C# 28, Rust 9, Python 17);\n     - the no-UI sentence.\n   - Final checks: `git status` clean, main merged, review `gh pr diff`, then `gh pr ready 108`.\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I was making atomic commits. Commit A is staged, with 7 files: SQLiteStorage.cs, Links.cs, Objects.cs, the experiments/sqlite_returning files, rust/src/links.rs, and only the BEGIN IMMEDIATE hunk of rust/src/objects.rs. It isn't committed yet. All other changes are unstaged or untracked, and all local checks passed on the full working tree.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Commit the staged changes, for example:\n   ```\n   git commit -m \"SQLite inserts read last_insert_rowid in both languages, transactions BEGIN IMMEDIATE\n\n   INSERT … RETURNING id makes SQLite inserts about 3× slower (experiments/sqlite_returning),\n   which slowed the C# SQLite creates from about 5 to 14 µs. Rust begins transactions\n   like Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.\"\n   ```\n   Then make commits B, C and D as listed in Pending Tasks, run `git pull --rebase`, and push to origin issue-107-bce58c21006b. After that, check CI and probe run 37228604862. This continues the user's request to \"double check that all CI/CD checks will pass like CodeFactor and so on, and that https://github.com/linksplatform/Comparisons.SQLiteVSDoublets/issues/107 is fully addressed.\"\n</summary>\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /home/box/.claude/projects/-tmp-gh-issue-solver-1791159559061/cc4b4be8-6a5c-4334-947a-5ea064ed7ac3.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."
